# Xbox 360 Native Linux Port

Turn a specific Xbox 360 executable revision into a reproducible Linux native
program backed by the required kernel, graphics, audio, storage and input
services. Translation success alone does not establish a playable port.

Read the target repository's agent instructions and identify the working
baseline before changing tools or launch flags. Pin title/media ID, executable
hash, title update, SDK/translator revision, compiler, standard library and
target CPU. Do not replace a working toolchain with a newer one incidentally.

For the IM2-derived mechanisms and their actual limits, read
[references/im2-lessons.md](references/im2-lessons.md) when diagnosing matching
audio, stencil, storage or toolchain symptoms. Its addresses and settings are
examples for that title, not defaults for another game.

## 1. Establish the inputs and toolchain

- Use the user's supplied extraction, retaining file names/case and required
  resources. Identify the entry XEX and any title DLLs or update patches.
  Keep original input immutable; place extracted images, translated game code,
  captured shaders, saves and raw samples outside version-controlled output.
- Inspect ELF target/CPU requirements and available RAM, disk, VRAM and
  thermals before expensive builds. Use the repository preflight if available,
  choose a bounded job count and keep long builds logged; avoid stacked builds.
- Separate translator and runtime responsibilities. XenonRecomp emits C++ and
  needs a compatible runtime. ReXGlue supplies its own codegen and runtime.
  Determine which output the native build actually consumes; do not link two
  translators' generated functions together or require a redundant translation.
- Check the installed tools' help and matching source/docs. Treat sample CLI
  flags, manifests and SDK filenames as versioned interfaces. Missing system
  development headers are a build dependency issue, not a reason to alter ABI
  checks or require privilege changes. Respect the environment's permissions.

## 2. Recover code faithfully

Recover entry points, imports, register save/restore helpers, function extents,
indirect-call targets and switch tables for this revision. Inspect disassembly
around an unresolved call or branch; a tail branch need not target a function
start. A pattern analyzer returning zero switch tables can mean its patterns
do not match this compiler, rather than the title having no switches.

Keep overrides and instrumentation in manifests, source hooks or maintained
patches so regeneration preserves them. Do not silence unsupported-instruction
warnings or add placeholder returns merely to reach zero errors. Validate
added arithmetic carry/overflow, condition-register behavior, vector element
ordering and floating-point semantics with meaningful edge cases.

Guest memory and command streams are big-endian where the guest interfaces
specify it. Distinguish native host values, guest addresses, packed structures
and GPU words; never decode a PM4 dump as little-endian solely because the
host is x86-64. Preserve guest pointer widths and hook calling conventions.

For an installed ReXGlue matching the checked CLI, an example project flow is:

```bash
# Set PORT_NAME, PORT_ROOT, GAME_ROOT, XEX and REXGLUE to this title's paths.
"$REXGLUE" init --project-name "$PORT_NAME" \
  --xex-path "$XEX" --project-root "$PORT_ROOT"
cd "$PORT_ROOT"
"$REXGLUE" codegen "${PORT_NAME}_manifest.toml"
```

Inspect the generated manifest's game root, entrypoint and hook sources before
building. The project name must also be suitable for generated C++ identifiers.
Use the pinned release's schema for missing-function/size overrides. Preserve
codegen diagnostics and unresolved-target reports; do not infer completeness
from generated file count. These roles and project commands are documented by
[ReXGlue](https://docs.rexglue.com/getting-started/).

## 3. Build against the real SDK ABI

Use the SDK's required Clang/C++ standard and standard-library major version.
For this project's prebuilt Linux SDK, that library is GCC 13 libstdc++.
Header compatibility at compile time is distinct from the minimum runtime
GLIBC/GLIBCXX symbol versions needed on another machine.

Check the compiler's actual include search paths. Use a valid GCC installation
directory when appropriate; for Homebrew's different layout, explicit verified
GCC 13 `-nostdinc++`/`-isystem` paths were required. Never change a macro or
remove the CMake guard to conceal incompatible C++ class layouts.

Inspect generated presets rather than assuming compiler paths exist:

```bash
cmake --list-presets
# Configure the selected Linux preset with the verified compiler/header paths.
cmake --build out/build/linux-amd64 --config Debug --parallel "$BUILD_JOBS"
```

Use the SDK integration helpers for host code and target linkage. In the IM2
build, host export registrations must remain in an OBJECT library because an
unreferenced STATIC archive can discard them. Verify the equivalent rule in
the target SDK. Prefer source hooks over hand-edited generated C++.

If only a prebuilt SDK is available, a narrowly scoped C/opaque-pointer
adapter can avoid exchanging SDK C++ layouts. Validate public symbol binding,
arguments, results and scope; do not deploy private offsets from a one-off
binary audit. Do not load a second copy of the SDK to find an original symbol.

## 4. Bring up the runtime one boundary at a time

Create a reproducible launcher with an explicit game root, writable user-data
root, actual module/library paths and console logging. Keep configuration and
saves separate from game assets. Start conservatively; retain working fixes
while diagnosing another subsystem. Read the installed SDK's real setting
registry rather than adding guessed flags from another emulator or fork.

| Symptom | Establish before patching |
| --- | --- |
| Black window / no menu | Window creation, presenter/swapchain, executable entry, guest threads, intro duration and actual asset opens |
| Red flashes / wrong lighting | Which render pass/transfer shader is wrong; source stencil/depth behavior; exact affected shader identity |
| Squealing / robotic audio | Guest callback cadence, queue credits, sample rate/block size, sample layout and repeat patterns before/after host conversion |
| Device missing / save fails | Content mount, real write result, event/IOSB state and the game's completion branch; successful host I/O is not game-level success |
| Controller absent | Actual loaded SDL version, enumeration and mapping, then Steam Input if applicable |
| Abort after quit | Timing relative to termination, interrupted waits and shutdown state; separate timeout-induced termination from a running-game failure |

Use bounded instrumentation and existing captures first. Do not autoplay
distorted samples or request repeated GPU/audio runs after a reported host
failure when an offline check can answer the question.

## 5. Preserve mechanics while adapting fixes

For audio, trace the title's registered render callback into its mixer. If
cadence is wrong, pace before mixing at `sample_rate / samples_per_block`,
retaining SDK back-pressure. Use monotonic per-client schedules, fractional
period accounting and stall rebasing without catch-up bursts. Do not equate
audio blocks with video FPS or double-pace a callback already fixed upstream.

For stencil transfer problems, validate SPIR-V and exact driver-bound shader
replacement before claiming a visual correction. Match precisely; do not
disable stencil broadly or copy a title's captured shader arrays to another.

For async saves, preserve the original write, real completion data and failure
paths. A successful write reported as pending is appropriate only when traced
game behavior requires it and narrow caller/size/event guards are established.
Do not globally transform successful writes into pending operations.

## 6. Validate and leave a usable port

Use deterministic or controlled tests for new translation semantics, adapter
forwarding, callback timing, symbol scope, failures and opt-outs. Loader-only
checks must not be described as a game run. Then validate menus and the
affected gameplay with actual output, including sound, controls, pause/resume,
save/relaunch/load and exit as relevant. Respect existing authorization and
the machine's demonstrated stability when choosing runtime tests.

Record executable/module hashes, SDK versions, timestamped logs, launch flags,
test results and their limits. Track translated, built, booting, menu reached,
gameplay working and progression persistence separately. A startup screenshot
does not measure audio quality, gameplay correctness or sustained FPS.

Deliver maintained manifests/hooks, build and launch instructions, compatible
modules, evidence and remaining issues. Preserve local user saves. Keep private
game assets/code/shader captures out of public commits and distinguish a private
user bundle from a releasable port. Leave Deck packaging as a separate stage
once the Linux baseline is understood.
